'''Opairatã''' or '''Opa-Iratã''' ({{Literal translation|Dwelling of the Serpent Spirits}}), is a {{convert|120|acre|ha}} [[Indigenous nations in Balisca|urban indigenous territory]] of the [[Kuarupíba]] indigenous peoples, located near what is now known as the [[Mokõi]] barrio of [[Trinacria]], [[Castejón]]. The Kuarupíba are building a highrise [[W:Housing estate|housing development]] on the land.

Long before the arrival of European settlers in the [[Alpujarra]] area in 1515, families from upper Kuarupíba villages would travel down to Opairatã, where the lands and waters were ideal for fishing, hunting, and harvesting traditional resources. There was an abundance of capybara, deer, catfish (specifically [[W:Pseudoplatystoma reticulatum|''surubí atigrado'']]), duck, [[W:Nothofagus dombeyi|coigüe]], and more. The Kuarupíba people built [[W:Oca (structure)|ocas]] and brought neighboring tribes together for [[W:Potlatch|apyká]] festivities. Opairatã continued to be an important hub for trade, commerce, social relationships, and cultural practices throughout the colonial era.

In 2019, the Kuarupíba Nation announced plans to build a housing development on this land, consisting of roughly 6,000 units. The tallest buildings in the development will be 56 stories and made of [[W:Engineered wood|mass timber]]. The development will also include 5,000-capacity [[W:bicycle parking station|bicycle parking station]], green space, and a [[W:Transit hub|transit hub]].

